# Global Rules

## Who You Are
You are a coding agent working on projects owned by George. You operate on a Mac Studio (Apple M2 Ultra, 64GB RAM) running macOS.

## Spec-Driven Development

All work follows a spec-driven workflow. Every project has a `SPEC.md` and a `TASKS.md` at its root.

### SPEC.md
The source of truth for what we are building. Contains:
- Project overview and goals
- Architecture decisions
- API contracts and data models
- Non-functional requirements (performance, security, etc.)

Never make architectural changes without updating SPEC.md first. If a task reveals the spec is wrong or incomplete, update the spec before proceeding.

### TASKS.md
The living task list. Format:

```markdown
# Tasks

## Current Sprint
- [ ] TASK-001: Short description
  - Status: in-progress | blocked | ready
  - Assignee: opencode | tars | george | unassigned
  - Branch: feature/task-001
  - Notes: Any context
- [x] TASK-002: Completed task
  - Status: done
  - Completed: 2026-03-14

## Backlog
- [ ] TASK-003: Future work
  - Status: ready
  - Priority: high | medium | low

## Done
- [x] TASK-002: What was done
  - Completed: 2026-03-14
  - PR: #42
```

### Workflow
1. **Before starting work**: Read SPEC.md and TASKS.md
2. **Pick a task**: Choose the highest-priority ready task assigned to you (or unassigned)
3. **Update status**: Mark it `in-progress` with your name as assignee
4. **Do the work**: Implement, test, commit
5. **Update on completion**: Move task to Done with completion date, PR/commit ref
6. **If blocked**: Mark `blocked` with notes explaining why

### Rules
- One task at a time
- Commit frequently with meaningful messages
- Run tests before marking done
- If a task is too large, break it into subtasks
- Keep TASKS.md as the single source of truth — any agent can read and update it

## Code Standards
- TypeScript: strict mode, no `any` unless justified
- Rust: `clippy` clean, `cargo fmt`
- Tests: write them. No excuses.
- British English in comments and docs
- Commit messages: imperative mood, concise (`Fix auth token refresh`, not `Fixed the auth token refresh issue`)

## Environment
- Mac Studio: Apple M2 Ultra, 64GB RAM, macOS
- LM Studio on localhost:1234 (local models)
- Git: configured, SSH keys set up
- Rust, Node.js, Python available

## Tools
- `eza` instead of `ls` (if available)
- `bat` instead of `cat` (if available)
- `rg` instead of `grep` (if available)
- `fd` instead of `find` (if available)

## Git — Push Policy

**Always push completed work.** After committing, push to the appropriate remote:

### Remotes
Each project has two remotes:
- **origin** — GitHub (grvhi/repo-name)
- **codecommit** — AWS CodeCommit (used for CI/CD pipelines)

### Push to CodeCommit
CodeCommit is the primary deployment remote. Always push here after commits:
```bash
git push codecommit main
```

The remote URL uses `git-remote-codecommit` and handles AWS auth automatically via the `constellation-master-readonly` profile. No manual credential setup needed.

### Push to GitHub
Push to GitHub for backup/collaboration:
```bash
git push origin main
```

### After making changes
```bash
git add -A
git commit -m "Concise imperative message"
git push codecommit main
git push origin main
```

**Do not ask George to push for you. Just push.**

## AWS Access

### Read-Only (use freely)
Use AWS Roles Anywhere via credential profiles. No tokens to manage — cert-based, always valid.

```bash
# Dev account (721990187164)
AWS_PROFILE=constellation-dev-readonly aws s3 ls

# Master/prod account (445849135789)
AWS_PROFILE=constellation-master-readonly aws s3 ls
```

Profiles are in `~/.aws/config`. These are **read-only** — you cannot modify AWS resources with these profiles (except CodeCommit push, which is allowed).

### Elevated Access (requires approval)
For write/admin operations, request elevated credentials via the approval gate Lambda:

1. **Request**: `curl -s "https://6iofuzbrnnqp2j4kvkbzvolzuu0ojyrm.lambda-url.ap-southeast-1.on.aws/?action=request&account=ACCOUNT_ID"`
   - ACCOUNT_ID: `445849135789` (master) or `721990187164` (dev)
2. **George approves**: He clicks the approval link and enters his TOTP
3. **Claim credentials**: `curl -s "https://6iofuzbrnnqp2j4kvkbzvolzuu0ojyrm.lambda-url.ap-southeast-1.on.aws/?action=claim&request_id=REQUEST_ID"`
4. The response contains KMS-encrypted temporary credentials — decrypt locally with the agent cert

**Never try to self-approve. Never bypass the approval workflow. Always tell George what you need elevated access for and why before requesting.**

### Account Reference
| Account | ID | Profile (read-only) | Purpose |
|---------|-----|---------------------|---------|
| Hub | 448387745454 | george-hub | Trust anchor, approval Lambda |
| Master | 445849135789 | constellation-master-readonly | Production, CodeCommit repos |
| Dev | 721990187164 | constellation-dev-readonly | Development |

### Common Commands
```bash
# List ECS services
AWS_PROFILE=constellation-dev-readonly aws ecs list-services --cluster constellation-dev-cluster --region ap-southeast-1

# Check CloudFront distributions
AWS_PROFILE=constellation-dev-readonly aws cloudfront list-distributions --query "DistributionList.Items[].{Id:Id,Domain:DomainName}"

# View CodePipeline status
AWS_PROFILE=constellation-master-readonly aws codepipeline get-pipeline-state --name constellation-pipeline --region ap-southeast-1

# Check ALB target health
AWS_PROFILE=constellation-dev-readonly aws elbv2 describe-target-health --target-group-arn TARGET_GROUP_ARN --region ap-southeast-1

# Check CodeBuild logs
AWS_PROFILE=constellation-master-readonly aws codebuild batch-get-builds --ids BUILD_ID --region ap-southeast-1
```
